WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.260
Credit recovery programs are on the rise.
These programs let high-school students

00:00:09.269 --> 00:00:12.990
who have fallen behind make up credits
needed to graduate, without retaking the

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:17.340
original course. As they've spread, so
have scandals where schools use lax

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.480
credit recovery programs to boost grad
rates. So what policies do school

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.500
districts have to ensure credit recovery
matches their academic standards? We

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.330
surveyed 200 to find out. Turns out
district policies prioritize flexibility

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:35.250
with minimal quality control. Most
programs are available online, all year,

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:40.350
to all students. Plus, most let students
pretest out of lessons with no minimum

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:45.149
seat time and no school-administered
exams. Schools face obvious pressure to

00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:49.230
raise grad rates, which is why school
districts need policies that answer the

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:53.940
question, "How will you ensure rigor in
your credit recovery programs?" When

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.020
districts have no good answer, students
suffer. To truly help struggling

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.550
students, districts need to craft
policies that prioritize rigor, or risk

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.480
being in the headline for the next
credit recovery scandal.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:11.720
Should credit recovery courses be just as rigorous as standard courses? Let us know in our poll.

00:01:11.729 --> 00:01:16.950
Also, let us know what other topics you'd
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:23.750
and be sure to like and subscribe for
more research and videos from AEI.

